# The TLS front for deploy/compose.local.yaml's `tls` profile. It exists for ONE reason, and the
# reason was measured rather than assumed.
#
# The console's session cookie is `__Host-tod_session` (internal/auth/session.go). Over plain HTTP:
#
#   Firefox 154   stores it on http://localhost
#   Chromium      does NOT — it stores a plain `Secure` cookie on localhost and refuses the
#                 `__Host-` prefix, which requires a CRYPTOGRAPHIC scheme; `localhost` is merely
#                 trustworthy, which is a weaker thing
#   Safari        does NOT — it refuses the `Secure` attribute over http outright
#
# and NONE of the three stores anything `Secure` when the origin is a LAN address rather than
# localhost. So a home server reached at http://192.168.x.x serves a console nobody can sign into,
# and the failure looks like "the login button does nothing". The full measurement is in
# docs/operations/deployment.md.
#
# `tls internal` issues from Caddy's own CA. Browsers do not trust it until you install the root —
# the runbook says how — but the scheme is https either way, which is the whole of what the cookie
# prefix requires.
#
# Nothing here is used on the droplet: Traefik terminates TLS there, from a real certificate.

{
	# No admin API. It is an unauthenticated control plane on a container that exists to hold one
	# reverse-proxy rule.
	admin off
}

{$TOD_DEPLOY_HOST:localhost}:8443 {
	tls internal

	# The console and the API are one origin, deliberately — the browser never makes a
	# cross-origin request and there is no CORS story anywhere in this project.
	reverse_proxy tod-serve:8080

	# HSTS is NOT set here, and that is the difference between this and the droplet. Sending it
	# from a host whose certificate a browser does not trust pins `localhost` to https for a year,
	# in that browser, for every other project that ever serves on it.
}
